  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Friday/... \nStrong, expansive ridging will remain nearly in place through next\nweek across the southwest and central United States with the \nanti-cyclone center rotating back from central Colorado into New \nMexico and Arizona. Early in the week, sufficient easterly flow \nwill prevail over the region juxtaposed with marginally supportive\nmixing ratios of 9-11 g/kg for scattered storms. There remains \nconsiderable uncertainty regarding a more preferred time frame for\nhigher POPS in any given location with mesoscale details likely \nbecoming more important towards convective evolution. Overall, \nthis appears to be a very typical monsoon pattern with alternating\nconvectively active and inactive days. However, by the latter \nhalf of the week, there are a handful of NAEFS members indicating \nnotable strengthening of the high pressure with 600dm H5 heights \nmaterializing and the center of the anticyclone shifting to over \nnorthern AZ/southern UT. It appears the NBM/WPC forecasts are \nlatching onto this scenario as POPs dwindle to much lower than \nexpected values given the ensemble mean synoptic pattern. The \nlarger impact of this outcome would be a return to major HeatRisk \nand extreme heat thresholds towards the end of the week, after the\nvery short-lived reprieve at the beginning of the week. As a\nresult, the current Extreme Heat Warning may actually be extended\nas one prolonged warning event.",
